g('how-to-import-into-india-step-by-step', 'import', 'How to import into India|step by step',
  'How to import goods into India: get an IEC, classify the product, check import policy, pay the supplier, file the bill of entry, pay duty and clear the cargo.',
  'Importing into India follows the same ten steps every time. Here they are in order, with the documents and the traps at each one.',
  [('Before you buy', ['Get an Import Export Code (IEC) from DGFT. No IEC, no import, with a few exceptions such as personal use.',
     'Find the eight-digit HSN code of the product. It decides the duty rate and whether the import is free, restricted or prohibited.',
     'Check the import policy and any licence, certificate or NOC needed (BIS, FSSAI, WPC, drug licence and so on). Do this before you pay an advance.']),
   ('Buy and ship', ['Agree the Incoterm and the price with the supplier. The Incoterm decides who pays freight and insurance, and so what goes into the customs value.',
     'Pay through an authorised dealer bank. Keep the remittance advice, because the bank and the customs officer may ask for it.',
     'Get the shipping documents from the supplier: commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading or airway bill, and a certificate of origin if you will claim a trade agreement rate.']),
   ('Clear the cargo', ['The shipping line or airline files the import manifest. Your customs broker then files the bill of entry on ICEGATE, usually before the ship arrives.',
     'You self-assess the duty. The system may pass the bill, ask for documents, or send the goods for examination.',
     'Pay the duty, get the out of charge order, collect the delivery order from the shipping line, pay the port or CFS charges and move the cargo out.']),
   ('Common traps', ['Wrong HS code. It changes duty and can trigger a demand notice years later.', 'Missing licence or certificate. The cargo sits at the port and demurrage runs every day.', 'Undervalued invoices. Customs checks values against its own database.'])],
  [('Can I import without an IEC?', 'In most cases no. A few imports, such as goods for personal use or some government and diplomatic cargo, do not need one.'),
   ('How long does import clearance take?', 'A clean bill of entry with no examination can clear in one to two days. Examination, queries or a missing certificate add days.'),
   ('Do I need a customs broker?', 'The law lets an importer file their own bill of entry, but almost everyone uses a licensed customs broker for speed and accuracy.')],
  ('customs-duty-calculator', 'Open the duty calculator'))
g('import-export-code-iec', 'import', 'Import Export Code (IEC)|what it is and how to get one',
  'What an Import Export Code (IEC) is, who needs it, how to apply on the DGFT portal, what you need, and how to keep it active.',
  'The IEC is a ten-character code from DGFT that lets a business import or export. It is linked to your PAN and, once issued, does not expire. But it must be kept updated.',
  [('Who needs an IEC', ['Any business that imports or exports goods or services for trade needs one, whether a proprietor, partnership, company or LLP.', 'Customs will not process a bill of entry or shipping bill without a valid IEC. Banks also ask for it for foreign payments.']),
   ('How to apply', ['Apply online on the DGFT portal. You need the PAN of the business, an address proof, a bank account proof (cancelled cheque or bank certificate) and a digital signature or Aadhaar based verification.', 'The application is usually processed within a few working days. A small government fee applies.']),
   ('Keeping it valid', ['DGFT requires every IEC holder to confirm or update the details once a year, in a window set by DGFT (it has been April to June). An IEC that is not updated can be made inactive.', 'Update the address, bank and branch details when they change. Customs and the bank both use these details.']),
   ('One IEC, many branches', 'One IEC covers the whole business. Branch addresses are added under the same code. A new PAN, such as a new company, needs a new IEC.')],
  [('Is an IEC needed to export services?', 'Not for most services. It is mainly for goods trade. If you claim some government benefits for services, check the rule.'),
   ('Does the IEC expire?', 'No. It is valid for life, but you must update it every year or it can be deactivated.'),
   ('Can a customs broker apply for me?', 'A broker or consultant can help, but the application is made in your name and the code belongs to you.')],
  ('document-checker', 'Open the document checker'))
g('bill-of-entry-explained', 'import', 'Bill of entry|what it is and how it works',
  'What a bill of entry is, the three types (home consumption, warehousing, ex-bond), what goes in it, how it is filed on ICEGATE and what happens after.',
  'The bill of entry is the document an importer files with Indian Customs to declare imported goods and pay duty. Without it nothing leaves the port.',
  [('What it contains', ['Importer and IEC, supplier, invoice number and value, HS code, quantity, country of origin, Incoterm, freight and insurance, and the duty worked out for each line.', 'It is filed as an electronic declaration on ICEGATE and is based on the commercial invoice, packing list and bill of lading or airway bill.']),
   ('Three types', [('table', ['Type', 'Used for'], [['Home consumption', 'Goods cleared into the Indian market after paying duty'], ['Into bond (warehousing)', 'Goods stored in a bonded warehouse with duty deferred'], ['Ex-bond', 'Goods taken out of the bonded warehouse and cleared on duty payment']])]),
   ('After you file', ['Under self assessment you work out the duty yourself. Customs risk management then decides: pass without checks, query, document check or physical examination.', 'Once duty is paid and the officer is satisfied, customs gives the out of charge order and the goods can be released.']),
   ('Corrections', 'Mistakes in a filed bill of entry can be amended before out of charge in many cases, and after in some. Amendments need a reason, documents and sometimes a fee, so get it right the first time.')],
  [('Who can file a bill of entry?', 'The importer, or a licensed customs broker acting for the importer.'),
   ('Can I file it before the ship arrives?', 'Yes. Prior (advance) filing is allowed and saves days at the port.'),
   ('What if I file late?', 'Late filing attracts charges and, if cargo stays too long, the custodian can move to sell it. File as soon as the manifest is ready.')],
  ('customs-late-charges-calculator', 'Check late-filing charges'))
g('documents-required-for-import-into-india', 'import', 'Documents required for import into India|checklist',
  'The documents needed to import into India: commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading or airway bill, IEC, bill of entry, certificates, and licences by product.',
  'Customs clears on paper, even though the filing is online. Keep this set ready before the cargo lands.',
  [('Always needed', ['Commercial invoice from the supplier, with full description, unit price, total and Incoterm.', 'Packing list matching the invoice and the boxes, weights and marks on the cargo.', 'Bill of lading (sea) or airway bill (air), plus the delivery order from the carrier.', 'IEC and the importer KYC. Bill of entry, filed by you or your broker.']),
   ('Needed for some goods', ['Certificate of origin, if you claim a lower duty under a trade agreement.', 'Test reports, BIS registration, FSSAI NOC, WPC approval, drug import licence, plant or animal quarantine certificates, depending on the product.', 'Insurance certificate, if the value includes it, and the freight invoice if freight is billed separately.']),
   ('Often asked for', ['Catalogue or technical literature for machinery and electronics. Proof of payment. Purchase order or contract. Letter of credit and bank documents if used.']),
   ('Common mistakes', 'Invoice and packing list that do not match, a different description on the bill of lading, missing weights, and a certificate of origin issued in the wrong form. Check the set before shipment, not at the port.')],
  [('Do I need originals?', 'The bill of lading originals (or telex release) are needed to collect the delivery order. Customs works mostly with scanned copies uploaded on ICEGATE.'),
   ('Is a proforma invoice enough?', 'No. Customs needs the final commercial invoice that matches the shipment.'),
   ('Who prepares the packing list?', 'The supplier or shipper prepares it. You can check it against our free document checker.')],
  ('document-checker', 'Open the document checker'))
g('import-policy-free-restricted-prohibited', 'import', 'Free, restricted and prohibited imports|how to check',
  'India classifies imports as free, restricted or prohibited in the ITC-HS. How to check your product and what to do when a licence or condition applies.',
  'Not every product can be imported freely. DGFT lists each HS code as free, restricted or prohibited in the ITC (HS) schedule.',
  [('The three groups', [('table', ['Group', 'Meaning'], [['Free', 'No licence needed, but other laws such as BIS or FSSAI may still apply'], ['Restricted', 'Needs an import licence, authorisation or condition to be met'], ['Prohibited', 'Cannot be imported, with narrow exceptions']])]),
   ('How to check', ['Take the eight-digit HSN code of your product. Look it up in the ITC-HS schedule on the DGFT site, or in our HSN code finder and duty calculator.', 'Read the policy condition and any note on that line. A restricted line can still be allowed against conditions such as a minimum import price or a registration.']),
   ('Other rules sit outside the policy', 'A free item may still need approval from another agency, for example a BIS licence for electronics, FSSAI for food, CDSCO for drugs, or an environment clearance for waste and scrap. These are separate from the DGFT policy.'),
   ('If you import against policy', 'Goods imported against the policy can be seized and confiscated, with penalty. Check before you order, not after the cargo lands.')],
  [('Where is the official list?', 'The ITC-HS schedule on the DGFT website. Policy is amended through DGFT notifications.'),
   ('Can a restricted item be imported?', 'Often yes, with a licence or if you meet the stated condition, such as import through a specific port or by a specific agency.'),
   ('Does the duty calculator show policy?', 'Yes. It shows the policy status for each HSN line in our data and a note when a condition applies.')],
  ('hsn-code-finder', 'Open the HSN code finder'))
g('bis-certification-for-imports', 'import', 'BIS certification for imports|CRS and QCO',
  'When imported products need BIS certification in India: Compulsory Registration Scheme, quality control orders, and what happens to non-compliant cargo.',
  'For many goods, India lets you import only if the product or the maker holds a BIS certificate. Cargo without it can be held at the port.',
  [('Two routes', ['Compulsory Registration Scheme (CRS) covers electronics and IT goods. The maker registers the product with BIS and the product carries the Standard Mark or registration number.', 'Quality Control Orders (QCOs) require certain products to follow an Indian Standard and carry the ISI mark. They cover many industrial and consumer goods, and the list changes.']),
   ('What importers must do', ['Check whether your HS code is under a QCO or CRS before you order.', 'Make sure the foreign maker holds the licence for that exact model.', 'Give customs the BIS licence or registration number in the bill of entry. Mark the product as required.']),
   ('If you are not compliant', 'Non-compliant cargo can be held, sent back, or in serious cases confiscated. Samples for testing may need a separate exemption from BIS.'),
   ('Stay current', 'QCOs are issued, paused and withdrawn often. Always check the latest BIS and DPIIT notices for your HS code before you commit to an order.')],
  [('How long does BIS registration take?', 'It depends on the product and the testing lab. Plan for weeks to a few months.'),
   ('Can I import samples without BIS?', 'Limited exemptions exist for samples, R and D and personal use, but they need BIS permission and conditions.'),
   ('Does the HSN finder show QCO?', 'It shows policy conditions in our data. For BIS, always confirm on the BIS portal.')],
  ('hsn-code-finder', 'Open the HSN code finder'))
g('customs-clearance-process-india', 'import', 'Customs clearance process in India|import, step by step',
  'How import customs clearance works in India from manifest to out of charge: filing, assessment, examination, duty payment and release from the port or CFS.',
  'Clearance is a chain: manifest, bill of entry, assessment, examination if chosen, duty payment, out of charge and delivery. Each link has a typical delay.',
  [('1. Manifest and arrival', 'The shipping line or airline files the import manifest. Your cargo gets a line number against the bill of lading or airway bill. The bill of entry cannot be matched until this is in.'),
   ('2. Filing and assessment', 'The broker files the bill of entry. Under self assessment you declare value, HS code and duty. The risk management system picks a path: auto pass, document check or examination.'),
   ('3. Examination', 'If selected, officers check documents and open packages at the port, CFS or container yard. You pay for the labour, the shifting and the storage that come with it.'),
   ('4. Duty and release', 'Duty is paid online. Customs then gives the out of charge order. You get the delivery order from the shipping line and arrange transport.'),
   ('5. Gate out', 'Pay terminal, CFS and other charges, get the gate pass and move the cargo. Return the empty container within the free days to avoid detention.')],
  [('Which step takes longest?', 'Usually missing documents or a physical examination, and weekends and holidays at the port.'),
   ('What is Out of Charge?', 'It is the customs order that releases the goods after duty is paid and the officer is satisfied.'),
   ('Can I track the bill of entry?', 'Yes. Use ICEGATE or ask your broker. Our ICEGATE status guide explains each status.')],
  ('icegate-status-guide', 'Open the ICEGATE status guide'))
g('customs-examination-and-out-of-charge', 'import', 'Customs examination and out of charge|what to expect',
  'What customs examination means in India: first check, 100 percent examination, sampling, and what out of charge means for your cargo.',
  'If customs picks your bill of entry for examination, the goods are checked before release. Knowing what happens helps you plan the days and the cost.',
  [('Why a bill is picked', 'The risk management system selects some bills at random and others for a reason: new importer, sensitive product, value that looks low, a past mismatch or a specific alert. It is not always a sign of fault.'),
   ('What the officer does', ['Checks that the goods match the invoice, packing list and bill of entry.', 'May weigh, count, open packages or draw a sample for testing.', 'May ask for more documents or raise a query on value or classification.']),
   ('Your costs', 'Labour for opening and closing, movement to the examination area, and extra storage days. These are charged by the terminal or CFS, not customs.'),
   ('Out of charge', 'After duty is paid and any query is closed, the officer records Out of Charge in the system. Only then can the carrier or terminal release the cargo.')],
  [('How long does examination take?', 'From a few hours to a few days, depending on the port, the load and any testing.'),
   ('Can I be present?', 'Your broker or an authorised person can attend the examination.'),
   ('What if the officer disagrees on value?', 'The officer can ask for proof and may reassess. You can ask for a speaking order and appeal if you disagree.')],
  ('icegate-status-guide', 'Open the ICEGATE status guide'))
g('courier-import-into-india', 'import', 'Courier import into India|limits and duty',
  'How courier imports work in India: what goes by courier, the value and document rules, duty, and when to move to a normal freight bill of entry.',
  'Small parcels can come into India by express courier with a simplified customs process. Larger or regulated goods need a regular bill of entry.',
  [('What courier import suits', 'Documents, samples, small value consignments and personal shipments. Customs sets limits on value and on the kind of goods that may use the courier route, and these limits change.'),
   ('Paperwork', ['Commercial invoice with value and description, the airway bill, and the importer KYC.', 'For some goods, an IEC, a permit or an NOC is still needed even for a small parcel.']),
   ('Duty', 'Duty is charged on value plus freight and insurance, at the rate for the product. The courier company pays on your behalf and bills you before delivery.'),
   ('When to switch', 'When the value is high, the goods are restricted, you import often, or you need to claim an input tax credit, use a normal air freight clearance with a regular bill of entry.')],
  [('Can I import a phone by courier?', 'You can, but the phone is dutiable and may need type approval. Check the current rules for your model.'),
   ('Why is my courier parcel held?', 'Usually a missing KYC, an unclear description, a restricted item or a value query.'),
   ('Does courier import give GST credit?', 'Often not in a form the buyer can claim. A regular bill of entry in the buyer name gives a cleaner record.')],
  ('landed-cost-calculator', 'Open the landed cost calculator'))
g('advance-bill-of-entry-prior-filing', 'import', 'Advance bill of entry|clear faster by filing early',
  'What an advance (prior) bill of entry is in India, when you can file it, and how it cuts port time and cost.',
  'You do not have to wait for the ship to arrive before filing. Prior filing lets customs assess the bill so the cargo can leave soon after it lands.',
  [('How it works', 'The bill of entry is filed before the vessel or aircraft arrives, once the shipping documents and manifest data are available. Assessment and duty payment can be done in advance.'),
   ('Why it matters', ['Fewer days at the terminal means lower storage and demurrage.', 'Queries can be solved while the ship is still at sea.', 'You can plan transport and the delivery order in advance.']),
   ('What you need', 'The invoice, packing list, bill of lading or draft, and the vessel and voyage details. Duty is paid, but the out of charge comes only after the cargo arrives and the manifest is in.'),
   ('Watch the rate', 'The exchange rate and the duty in force on the date of filing or arrival can apply. Check which date your cargo falls under.')],
  [('How early can I file?', 'The rules allow filing before arrival of the vessel or aircraft. Your broker will know the current limit.'),
   ('Does prior filing guarantee quick release?', 'No. Selection for examination and missing documents can still delay it.'),
   ('Is it risky?', 'Not if the documents are final. If values or quantities change after you file, an amendment will be needed.')],
  ('customs-duty-calculator', 'Open the duty calculator'))
g('provisional-assessment-customs', 'import', 'Provisional assessment|when you cannot finalise duty',
  'What provisional assessment means in Indian customs: when it is used, what a bond and a bank guarantee do, and how it is closed out.',
  'Sometimes duty cannot be fixed on the day: a test report is pending, the price is not final, or classification is under dispute. Provisional assessment lets you clear the goods first.',
  [('When it is used', ['Waiting for a chemical or technical test report.', 'The price depends on a later event, such as a formula or a final settlement.', 'An unresolved classification or valuation question.']),
   ('How it works', 'You request provisional assessment. Customs sets a provisional duty, you give a bond and security, often a bank guarantee, and the goods are released.'),
   ('Closing it', 'When the report or price is final, customs finalises the assessment. You pay the extra duty, or get a refund, and the bond is released.'),
   ('Plan for the cost', 'The bank guarantee has a fee and ties up credit. Keep track of the case so it is not left open, because interest and a demand can follow.')],
  [('Is provisional the same as under protest?', 'No. Payment under protest is a way to dispute a final assessment. Provisional assessment is a temporary one.'),
   ('How long can it stay open?', 'Customs expects it to be finalised without delay. Ask your broker or consultant to follow up.'),
   ('Do I need a guarantee?', 'Usually a bond with security. The amount depends on the difference in duty at stake.')],
  ('customs-duty-calculator', 'Open the duty calculator'))
g('ex-bond-bill-of-entry-bonded-warehouse', 'import', 'Bonded warehouse and ex-bond bill of entry|how duty deferral works',
  'How importing into a bonded warehouse works in India, the into-bond and ex-bond bills of entry, and when duty deferral helps cash flow.',
  'A bonded warehouse lets you import the goods first and pay duty only when you take them out for sale in India. That helps cash flow, if the numbers work.',
  [('The two bills of entry', ['The into-bond bill of entry brings the goods into the licensed warehouse without paying duty at that time.', 'The ex-bond bill of entry clears them, in full or in part, for home use, and duty is paid then.']),
   ('Who it suits', 'Importers who sell in lots, hold stock for a long period, or re-export part of it. If you re-export from the bond, duty is not paid on that part.'),
   ('Points to check', ['Warehouse licence and the custodian charges, which are paid in addition to duty.', 'The period of bonding and the interest that can apply on duty after a time limit.', 'The rate and exchange rate on the date of ex-bonding, which can differ from the date of import.']),
   ('Records', 'Keep stock and movement records. Customs audits bonded stock and any shortage is charged duty.')],
  [('Can goods be sold while in bond?', 'Only by transfer or sale in bond, with customs permission. Normally they are cleared first by an ex-bond bill of entry.'),
   ('Which duty applies, import or ex-bond date?', 'In general the rate on the date of the ex-bond bill of entry applies. Your broker will confirm.'),
   ('Is there a time limit?', 'Yes. Beyond the period allowed, interest can apply and in the end the goods may be sold.')],
  ('bonded-warehousing', 'See our bonded warehousing'))
g('fssai-import-clearance', 'import', 'FSSAI clearance for food imports|what importers need',
  'How food imports are cleared in India: FSSAI import licence, labelling, shelf life, sampling at the port and what happens to non-conforming food.',
  'Food and beverage imports need FSSAI clearance in addition to customs. It is a separate approval and it happens at the port.',
  [('Before you import', ['Hold an FSSAI import licence or registration for the business, and an IEC.', 'Make sure the product is allowed under Indian food standards and that additives and claims are within the limits.', 'Check labelling: the pack must carry the information required by the labelling rules, with the importer details.']),
   ('At the port', 'The Authorised Officer of FSSAI checks the documents and may sample the cargo. Clearance is by an NOC. Cargo is held until the sample result is in.'),
   ('Shelf life and labels', 'Imported food must usually have a minimum shelf life left on arrival, and labels must be correct at the time of import. Re-labelling is allowed only as FSSAI permits.'),
   ('If the sample fails', 'The cargo can be rejected, with a re-export or destruction order. Keep the supplier test reports ready to reduce the risk.')],
  [('Do I need FSSAI for a small food sample?', 'Samples also need approval or an exemption under FSSAI rules. Check before shipping.'),
   ('How long does sampling take?', 'It varies by lab and product, typically several days.'),
   ('Does FSSAI replace the customs clearance?', 'No. Customs and FSSAI are two separate clearances. Both are needed.')],
  ('document-checker', 'Open the document checker'))
